# Where a release goes.
#
# The fennec library (cnf/ext/fennec.bnd, -library: fennec) brings the mechanics: DO_RELEASE
# decides the version bnd stamps on every bundle (releng/snapshot.bnd: ${tstamp}-SNAPSHOT;
# releng/release.bnd: the bare version), '-pom: true' and '-maven-release: pom' the Maven side,
# and — only when GPG_PASSPHRASE is set and maven-central is true — releng/central.bnd: the
# 'Sonatype' repository of bnd 7.4's Sonatype Central Portal support (stagingUrl =
# production.portal.central.sonatype.com, sonatypeMode = autopublish, signed with
# '-maven-release: pom, sign'), which then becomes '-releaserepo'.
#
# This workspace adds the DIM Nexus beside it. '-releaserepo' is set here outright (what the
# workspace sets wins over the library), so the Nexus is on every release and Sonatype only
# when the library has configured it:
#   snapshot branch (no GPG):  -releaserepo = Release            → dim-snapshot
#   main branch (GPG + Portal): -releaserepo = Sonatype, Release → Maven Central and dim-release
# Which Nexus URL is used follows from the version: a -SNAPSHOT goes to snapshotUrl. (Not a
# '-releaserepo.nexus' suffix key: merged with the library's 'Release' that gives 'Release~',
# a name no repository has.)
#
# Credentials are not in the repository. They are the Jenkins agent's, as with the old DIM
# library that released the old weather workspace: bnd reads ~/.bnd/connection-settings.xml and
# ~/.m2/settings.xml (the leading '-' makes a missing file no error), where a <server> whose id
# is the host carries the login — devel.data-in-motion.biz for the Nexus,
# production.portal.central.sonatype.com for the Portal (new host: the old library spoke to
# ossrh-staging-api.central.sonatype.com, so that entry has to be added once). The key is set
# outright, not as a suffix, so that the library's releng/central.bnd — which would replace the
# list with its own sonatype-settings.xml and thereby switch the agent's files off — does not
# apply. The two inline servers are for a CI that hands the logins over in the environment
# instead (NEXUS_USERNAME/NEXUS_PASSWORD, CS_USERNAME/CS_PASSWORD, the names emf.osgi's GitHub
# workflows use); unset, they carry no credentials and do nothing. The GPG passphrase arrives in
# GPG_PASSPHRASE (the Jenkinsfile maps the Jenkins' GNUPG_PASSPHRASE onto it).
# See docs/12-build-and-release.md and the Jenkinsfile.

-plugin.7.Release: \
	aQute.bnd.repository.maven.provider.MavenBndRepository;\
		releaseUrl  = https://devel.data-in-motion.biz/nexus/repository/dim-release/;\
		snapshotUrl = https://devel.data-in-motion.biz/nexus/repository/dim-snapshot/;\
		name        = 'Release'

-releaserepo: ${if;${env;GPG_PASSPHRASE;};${if;${maven-central};Sonatype,Release;Release};Release}

-connection-settings: \
	-bnd,\
	-maven,\
	server; \
		id=devel.data-in-motion.biz; \
		username="${env;NEXUS_USERNAME;}"; \
		password="${env;NEXUS_PASSWORD;}",\
	server; \
		id=production.portal.central.sonatype.com; \
		username="${env;CS_USERNAME;}"; \
		password="${env;CS_PASSWORD;}"

# Signing, as the old DIM library did it on this Jenkins: the keyring is the agent's (the old
# setup pointed gpg at /var/jenkins_home/.gnupg; gpg honours GNUPGHOME, which the Jenkinsfile
# sets), the passphrase arrives in GPG_PASSPHRASE (the library's name; the Jenkinsfile maps the
# old GNUPG_PASSPHRASE onto it). The library's own gpg line insists on GPG_KEY_ID; here the
# --local-user is only given when a key id is set, otherwise gpg takes its default key — as the
# old setup did.
gpg: gpg ${if;${env;GPG_KEY_ID;};--local-user ${env;GPG_KEY_ID}} --pinentry-mode loopback --batch
